WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.780
Authentication verifies who or what is calling the API.

2
00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:16.760
Python services may use API keys, bearer tokens, signed tokens, OAuth and OpenID Connect concepts, service accounts, workload identity, or session-based approaches for browser clients.

3
00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:22.780
Each pattern has tradeoffs, but none of them removes the need for careful validation and follow-on authorization.

4
00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:28.720
An API should know how the caller identity was established and what confidence that identity carries.

5
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:33.080
Signed token validation should check more than token presence.

6
00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:43.100
At a high level, the service may need to verify signature integrity, issuer, audience, expiration, not-before time, intended use, and relevant claims.

7
00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:47.440
A token meant for one service should not automatically be accepted by another.

8
00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:52.420
An expired token should not remain useful simply because it is well formed.

9
00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:57.080
A token with missing context should not be treated as a blank check.

10
00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:01.760
API keys and service accounts also require lifecycle thinking.

11
00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:07.060
They should have owners, scopes, storage rules, rotation plans, and logging.

12
00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:15.160
Long-lived credentials increase exposure when they leak, so teams should prefer narrow capability and operational visibility.

13
00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:26.780
Revocation concepts matter because identity is not only about initial issuance; the system needs a way to respond when access should end, credentials are replaced, or a service relationship changes.

14
00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:32.020
Authentication must be followed by authorization, logging, and monitoring.

15
00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:36.680
A valid token may identify a user who cannot access the requested object.

16
00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:43.260
A valid service account may be allowed to read one dataset but not trigger administrative work.

17
00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:46.800
Token presence is only the beginning of the decision.

18
00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:55.860
Secure APIs validate the token, understand the subject, enforce permissions, record meaningful events, and watch for unusual usage patterns.
